<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Kotah State</h1><p class="st-meta">1631 CE – 1948 CE (approx.) · capital Kota</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Hada Chauhan Rajput state on the Chambal river, separated from Bundi under Shah Jahan and famed for its school of painting.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Kota was separated from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bundi-state">Bundi State</a> in 1631, when the Mughal emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shah-jahan">Shah Jahan</a> granted it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madho-singh-of-kota">Madho Singh</a>, a son of Rao <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ratan-singh-of-bundi">Ratan Singh of Bundi</a>, as a reward for service. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hada-chauhans">Hada</a> rulers continued as Mughal mansabdars.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Maharaos ruled the fertile <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chambal-river">Chambal</a> valley. In the late 18th century the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalim-singh-jhala">Zalim Singh Jhala</a> dominated the state, and in 1838 the British created <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jhalawar-state">Jhalawar</a> for his descendants out of Kota lands. The court fostered the Kota school of painting, noted for its hunting scenes. It was a seventeen-gun salute state.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Kota joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> union in 1948, and Maharao <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhim-singh-ii-of-kotah">Bhim Singh II</a> became its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajpramukh">Rajpramukh</a>. Kota is now a major city of Rajasthan.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kotah-state.jpg" alt="Kotah State" width="320" height="357"><figcaption>Image: Robert Alfers · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kotah.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1631 CE – 1948 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kota</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Madho Singh, Bhim Singh II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hadoti</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kota, Kotah</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1790&amp;focus=kotah-state" aria-label="Kotah State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6690 1251 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kotah State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7010 1507L7003 1498M7010 1507L7018 1385M7010 1507L7364 1601M7010 1507L7364 1602M7010 1507L6996 1446M7010 1507L7023 1528"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7003 1498h0M7018 1385h0M7364 1601h0M7364 1602h0M6996 1446h0M7023 1528h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7010 1507h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7010 1507h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kota. <a href="/atlas?year=1790&amp;focus=kotah-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
